WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:01.920
This particular piece is important.

00:01.920 --> 00:04.680
I think it's been used a lot here.

00:04.680 --> 00:06.840
We use it a lot.

00:06.840 --> 00:10.280
I use it in classes, in coursework,

00:10.280 --> 00:13.680
when we're trying to think about things around policing

00:13.680 --> 00:14.880
specifically.

00:14.880 --> 00:16.480
But it's especially important to me too,

00:16.480 --> 00:20.360
because the first place I used was in Durham.

00:20.360 --> 00:25.960
It was the kind of the take back the community pushing back

00:25.960 --> 00:27.520
against the policing initiatives that

00:27.520 --> 00:31.600
were being led around that, the night out for safety and democracy,

00:31.600 --> 00:33.360
and Durham's close to home here.

00:33.360 --> 00:37.320
So we kind of, to me, it's always been in some ways connected

00:37.320 --> 00:41.160
to where we are in an interesting way and thinking about it.

00:41.160 --> 00:43.800
But it's hanging in my office.

00:43.800 --> 00:46.040
I want my students to see it when they come in

00:46.040 --> 00:52.320
that what safety looks like is, in fact, not police, but this.

00:52.320 --> 00:54.360
So I just walk us through this one.

00:55.360 --> 01:03.360
Well, this was the first image I made that blew up before Marsha.

01:03.360 --> 01:07.360
And it was a commission from Ella Baker Center.

01:07.360 --> 01:16.360
And the reason it blew up, and this taught me about timing,

01:16.360 --> 01:20.360
because it just happened to be released,

01:21.360 --> 01:27.360
when the verdict was coming down around Trayvon's murder

01:27.360 --> 01:31.360
and the national outrage about that and grief.

01:31.360 --> 01:37.360
And I think the very powerful words that actually, I think it was in a meeting

01:37.360 --> 01:39.360
with me and Zach and Emeraldis.

01:39.360 --> 01:42.360
I think Emeraldis De Hussu's molestine might have come up with these words.

01:42.360 --> 01:46.360
And now I'm like, I wish I had credited her.

01:46.360 --> 01:49.360
I mean, used with permission, but I feel very aware

01:50.360 --> 01:53.360
of wanting to be abundance in my crediting.

01:53.360 --> 01:55.360
So us too.

01:55.360 --> 01:57.360
Yes, yes.

01:57.360 --> 02:09.360
And what it meant to show a black young man in a hoodie at that moment.

02:09.360 --> 02:12.360
Okay.

02:13.360 --> 02:18.360
So the text says, I don't watch my neighbors.

02:18.360 --> 02:20.360
I see them.

02:20.360 --> 02:25.360
We make our community safer together against a blue starry sky

02:25.360 --> 02:29.360
with a drawing, implying apartment building.

02:29.360 --> 02:38.360
And you see Asian elder grandma in the middle ground leaning on her window sill.

02:39.360 --> 02:46.360
And a porch stoop with a brown skin woman, a hijab reading a book to her child.

02:46.360 --> 02:57.360
And in the foreground is a young black young man on, you know, scraper bike wearing a hoodie.

02:57.360 --> 02:59.360
This was earlier than the Marcha poster.

02:59.360 --> 03:03.360
So I think I was still I worked as a designer primarily for 10 years.

03:04.360 --> 03:09.360
So this was sort of before I started doing hand lettering.

03:09.360 --> 03:12.360
And I think this poster is more simple.

03:12.360 --> 03:15.360
And some ways than some of my other images.

